-- title:
--   Newform level equals local newvector conductor at each prime
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a nonzero natural number and let $g$ be a weight-two cusp form for $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform in the sense of the project: $g$ is a normalised eigenform (its $q$-expansion coefficients satisfy $a_1=1$, $a_{mn}=a_m a_n$ for coprime $m,n$, the recursion $a_{p^{r+2}}=a_p a_{p^{r+1}}-p\,a_{p^r}$ at primes $p\nmid M$ and $a_{p^{r+2}}=a_p a_{p^{r+1}}$ at primes $p\mid M$), and for no divisor $M'\mid M$ with $M'\neq M$ does there exist a normalised eigenform of level $M'$ whose $\ell$-th coefficient agrees with that of $g$ for all primes $\ell\nmid M$. Let $\Phi$ be a nonzero complex-valued function on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ of the adeles of $\mathbb{Q}$ which is an adelic lift of $g$: it is invariant under left translation by the image of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q})$, invariant under right translation by the image under [`AdelicDock.finEmbed`](def/AdelicDock_LocalEmbedding.html#L145) of the finite level-one subgroup attached to the ideal $(M)$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, and at every $h$ whose finite component is trivial and whose archimedean component [`LanglandsTunnell.ratArchGL2 h`](def/LanglandsTunnell_DeltaLift.html#L16) lies in $\mathrm{GL}_2^+(\mathbb{R})$ its value is the weight-two slash action of that archimedean matrix applied to $g$, evaluated at $i$. Then for every prime $q$, the space [`LocalNewvector.AdelicSpan`](def/LocalNewvector_AdelicSpanCarrier.html#L82) $\Phi$ — the complex span of the adelic translates of $\Phi$, with its $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_q)$-action — has newvector conductor exactly $v_q(M)$: the submodule fixed by [`LocalNewvector.padicK1`](def/LocalNewvector_CongruenceSubgroupK1.html#L161) $q\,v_q(M)$ is nonzero, and the submodule fixed by [`LocalNewvector.padicK1`](def/LocalNewvector_CongruenceSubgroupK1.html#L161) $q\,m$ is zero for every $m<v_q(M)$.
--
--   This is the equality, one prime at a time, of the level of a weight-two newform with the conductor of the local representation generated by an adelic lift of it, in the form due to Casselman. It is used downstream in the analysis of the local representation at primes $q$ with $q^2\mid M$, for instance in ruling out unramified principal series and in producing quadratic twists of exponent one.
